# Requests for comments

<Aside type="caution" title="A concept">

These RFCs show how researchers and providers could agree on what research needs from compute. Send feedback to
#ask-compute or the [compute needs survey](/survey/).

</Aside>

Compute is central to research. RFCs are how the research community and providers agree on what
researchers need from it.

<RfcDiagram class="not-prose my-8" />

## Open RFCs

<!-- md:replace rfc-list -->
<RfcList rfcs={openRfcs(3)} />
<!-- md:end -->

See [all RFCs](/rfc/all/), with their status, or follow changes in the [Atom feed](/rfc/feed.xml).

## How RFCs work

| Who | Does |
| --- | --- |
| Researchers | Say what they need: in the [compute needs survey](/survey/), at [events](/events/), in the P1 consortium. Nobody has to write an RFC. |
| Research engineers | Draft RFCs from that feedback, with an open network of DevOps, MLOps and domain experts. |
| [HPC governance board](/about/#hpc-governance-board) | Decides each RFC. In the P1 consortium it has one PI per partner institution, and they carry the mandate regarding compute decisions. |
| Providers | Review drafts and meet the requirements. |

Each RFC covers one concern.

## Why RFCs

- **Listening:** the needs of researchers set the direction.
- **Expectations:** providers know what access and service the research community expects.
- **Open knowledge:** no standard depends on a single consultant or vendor.
- **Both ways:** the research community trains researchers to use shared compute well through teaching, events and innovation.
- **Best effort:** nothing here is enforced. It works because everything is public.

